1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,620
[Narrator] In 1498, a Portuguese explorer named Vasco da Gama sailed into the port of

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:10,580
[Narrator] Calicut on the southwestern coast of India. He had spent nearly a year at sea.

3
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:13,820
[Narrator] He had lost a third of his crew to scurvy and storms.

4
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:18,129
[Narrator] And when the local rulers of Calicut asked him what he had come all this way

5
00:00:18,129 --> 00:00:22,989
[Narrator] for, his answer was simple: pepper, cinnamon, cloves. That was it.

6
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:28,320
[Narrator] Three dried plants, three ingredients you can buy today at any grocery store for a few dollars.

7
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:33,179
[Narrator] Da Gama had risked everything, crossed 17,000 miles of open ocean,

8
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:36,289
[Narrator] and watched dozens of men die, all for seasoning.

9
00:00:36,289 --> 00:00:39,579
[Narrator] That was 1498, but by 1502,

10
00:00:39,579 --> 00:00:43,369
[Narrator] da Gama returned to the same coast with 20 warships and cannons.

11
00:00:43,369 --> 00:00:47,969
[Narrator] And he burned an entire merchant vessel full of Muslim traders alive in the

12
00:00:47,969 --> 00:00:52,570
[Narrator] harbor of Calicut. Over 400 men, women, and children were killed.

13
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:55,520
[Narrator] His reason was the same as before: spices.

14
00:00:55,570 --> 00:01:00,170
[Narrator] So, how did a handful of dried bark and flower buds become valuable enough to

15
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:05,370
[Narrator] justify mass murder, fund entire empires, and redraw the map of the world?

16
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:09,130
[Narrator] The answer starts thousands of years before da Gama was born,

17
00:01:09,130 --> 00:01:12,840
[Narrator] in the deserts of ancient Egypt along the banks of the Nile River.

